# The build context is streamed to the Docker daemon on every `make build`, so
# what is *not* listed here matters more than what is. After `make provision`
# the repository root holds ./models (~7.5 GB of weights) and ./data (the
# acquired corpus). Both are bind-mounted at run time (compose.yaml) and must
# never be baked into an image — an image carrying ./data would ship corpus
# text, which is the one thing ADR-0014 exists to prevent.
#
# Deny everything, then re-include exactly what a Dockerfile COPYs. An
# allow-list fails loudly — add a COPY without a line here and the build stops
# on a missing file — where a deny-list fails silently, letting the next large
# gitignored tree at the root back into the context unnoticed.
*

# services/gateway/Dockerfile
!go.mod
!go.sum
!services/gateway
# The generated Go stubs, for the reason `services/catena/gen` is here: they are
# committed (ADR-0022) so the image needs no `buf` and no `make proto` first. The
# gateway imports them directly -- `gen/berean/v1` is the answer object it
# verifies -- so without this line the build fails on a missing package.
!gen
# The profile documents. They are the tradition's doctrinal commitments and the
# gateway loads one per invocation, so an image without them can answer nothing.
# Baked in rather than mounted, for the reason catena's source is: a container
# started against a stale image would resolve a profile the working tree no
# longer holds, and `make build` is already a prerequisite of every target that
# runs one.
!profiles

# services/catena/Dockerfile — manifest and lock first, then source, so the
# dependency layer caches independently of the source layer.
!services/catena/pyproject.toml
!services/catena/uv.lock
!services/catena/src
# The generated stubs are committed rather than built here (ADR-0022). The
# image needs no `buf` and the build reaches no network beyond the base image,
# which is what makes `docker compose up` work on a clean clone -- catena is in
# the default `up` as of Task 7.
!services/catena/gen
